# The container both workflows run the Python suite in, built here instead of being assembled
# from scratch inside the step on every run.
#
# WHAT THIS BUYS. Assembling the same environment inside the step instead — which is what this
# replaced — means one `apt-get install` for the kernel's libraries and one `pip install -r
# requirements-dev.txt`, some 430 MB of wheels with the OCCT ones among them, on EVERY run:
# 112 s of pip and 28 s of apt out of a 299 s step, and none of it
# depends on the commit under test. Here both are layers keyed by the files named below, so a run
# whose dependencies have not moved reuses them and installs nothing at all — 1 s
# against 252 s for a build that has to do the work.
#
# THE RUNNER'S DAEMON IS PERSISTENT, which is the fact that makes the reuse possible at all —
# and the same fact is why the build in the workflow carries `--pull`: a python:3.11-slim pulled
# once would otherwise sit in that cache for months and the suite would run on an unpatched
# base. Reuse and staleness come from the one property, and `--pull` together with the hashed
# tag is how both halves are handled.
#
# THE TAG IS A HASH OF THIS FILE AND THE TWO REQUIREMENTS FILES, computed in the step that
# builds it. That is what makes the reuse SAFE rather than merely cheap: any edit here, or to
# either of those files, changes the tag, so a stale image cannot be picked up under a name that
# says it is current. Do not replace it with a floating `:ci` — that name would go on meaning
# "whatever was built last", which on a persistent daemon is a suite running against somebody
# else's branch's dependencies.
#
# It is built by both workflows' "Run the test suite in a container" step — one byte-identical
# body — and by nothing else, and it is never pushed anywhere: the tag exists on the runner's
# daemon only. One image per distinct set of requirements accumulates there — 1.9 GB of wheels
# over a 317 MB library layer, by `docker history` — and like the dangling bases `--pull` leaves
# behind, that is the runner host's periodic cleanup to reclaim rather than this repository's.
#
# NOT the image the service ships in — that is the Dockerfile at the repository root, which
# installs requirements.lock with `--require-hashes` and carries no test dependency at all. This
# one installs the hand-written requirements-dev.txt (which pulls in requirements.txt), exactly
# as the step it replaces did: what the suite runs against is unchanged by moving it in here.
#
# THE SAME BASE AS THAT IMAGE, python:3.11-slim. Testing under a different interpreter than the
# one that ships is the gap the whole arrangement exists to close — a suite green on one python
# and an image running another — so change one, change both.
FROM python:3.11-slim

# GIT, for exactly one test: tests/test_no_conflict_markers.py enumerates the tracked files with
# `git ls-files`, and python:3.11-slim carries no git at all. The workspace arrives as a tar with
# `.git` inside it precisely so that test can ask the index rather than the working tree; without
# git the test does not check less, it dies with FileNotFoundError.
#
# THE CAD KERNEL'S SYSTEM LIBRARIES are the rest of the line, and they are the same list the
# root Dockerfile installs — read the derivation THERE and do not repeat it here; the two lists
# are compared by tests/test_ci_kernel_libs.py. Without them `import cadquery` dies with
# `ImportError: libGL.so.1` and about ninety tests quietly become skips (measured: 94 of them
# against 4 with the libraries, issue #27) — so a change to src/cadbuild/ that moves the
# payload's shape would ride through a green CI while vitest went on checking the browser half
# against a fixture no build produces any more. `curl` and `gosu` are on the Dockerfile's line
# and deliberately not here: they are the shipped image's runtime tools, the healthcheck's and
# the entrypoint's, and nothing in the suite runs either.
#
# ONE `RUN` with the apt lists dropped in the SAME layer, for the reason the Dockerfile spells
# out: removing them later would shrink nothing, the lists being already committed above.
RUN apt-get update \
    && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
        git \
        libgl1 \
        libx11-6 \
        libexpat1 \
        libxext6 \
        libxrender1 \
        libsm6 \
        libice6 \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# The workspace lands in /src as a tar unpacked by root, and git refuses a repository whose files
# belong to somebody else — `detected dubious ownership`, an error rather than a warning. On the
# runner both ends are root and this changes nothing; it is baked in for the by-hand run of the
# same command in a developer's checkout, where the files are the developer's and the container
# is root, and it belongs in the image rather than in the step because it is a property of where
# the tree is unpacked.
RUN git config --global --add safe.directory /src

# Housekeeping: pip's version-check notice is noise in a build log, which is read only when
# something else has already gone wrong.
ENV PIP_DISABLE_PIP_VERSION_CHECK=1

# BOTH FILES, because requirements-dev.txt opens with `-r requirements.txt` and pip resolves that
# beside the file it is reading. requirements.lock is deliberately not here: nothing in this
# image reads it, which is also why it stays out of the tag's hash — a re-locked dependency tree
# that nobody has asked this container to install must not invalidate it.
#
# They arrive through a tar built in the step from exactly these two files plus this one, not
# through the repository's build context: .dockerignore does not apply to a tar on stdin, and a
# context of three files is also a statement about what CAN affect this image.
COPY requirements.txt requirements-dev.txt /reqs/
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r /reqs/requirements-dev.txt
